    content: "# What Scientists Mean by Matter\n\n*Grade 6 Science · Unit 1 of 10 · Properties of Matter · Lesson 1*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** define matter as anything that has mass and takes up space; separate matter from energy and ideas.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nTrue or false (guess OK): “drawing force arrows that ignore direction or equal-length action–reaction pairs” — write why you agree or disagree in one sentence.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 1 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **What Scientists Mean by Matter** — sits inside **Properties of Matter**. Grade 6 science is not a pile of trivia; it is a set of models that help us explain patterns in matter, energy, Earth systems, and living things. A correct vocabulary word with no model or evidence is weaker than a clear diagram plus a checked explanation.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading weather maps, judging product claims, understanding local land and water, or talking about health and stewardship. At Prosper Prep we also connect careful science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show your model, cite evidence, and revise when new data arrives.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **mixture** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **particle** — pair it with an example from a creek-bank soil sample.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “I observed… which supports…” over “because science says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a creek-bank soil sample. We want to understand something connected to “What Scientists Mean by Matter.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Particle sketch, force diagram, Earth–Sun–Moon model, food web, data table, or systems map — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect observations to the definition of mixture. If numbers help (for example relative amounts 7 and -3), label units.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: drawing force arrows that ignore direction or equal-length action–reaction pairs?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a creek-bank soil sample and labeled mixture and particle. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (drawing force arrows that ignore direction or equal-length…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “What Scientists Mean by Matter” but switch the context to a basketball on the gym floor.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** drawing force arrows that ignore direction or equal-length action–reaction pairs.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nPhysical science ideas about matter and change set up later force, energy, and Earth-system explanations.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a creek-bank soil sample, restate today’s goal (“What Scientists Mean by Matter”) in your own words, then list two observations you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Define matter as anything that has mass and takes up space; … Observations should be measurable or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **mixture** and **particle** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a lab notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because of science” with no model. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear models + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “What Scientists Mean by Matter” to a short scenario about a kitchen density demo with oil and water. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 6, 4, 21.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “What Scientists Mean by Matter” to a short scenario about a Prosper Prep science lab station. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 6, 7, 40.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “What Scientists Mean by Matter” to a short scenario about a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 6, 11, 19.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “What Scientists Mean by Matter” to a short scenario about a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 6, 14, 38.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “What Scientists Mean by Matter” to a short scenario about a school recycling audit. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 15, 9, 17.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “What Scientists Mean by Matter” to a short scenario about a basketball on the gym floor. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 15, 13, 36.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your model and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Define matter as anything that has mass and takes …\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Define matter as anything that has mass and takes …\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Define matter as anything that has mass and takes …\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Define matter as anything that has mass and takes …\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Define matter as anything that has mass and takes …\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Define matter as anything that has mass and takes …\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “What Scientists Mean by Matter” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: drawing force arrows that ignore direction…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “What Scientists Mean by Matter” using a creek-bank soil sample. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
