    content: "# Conservation of Mass Intro\n\n*Grade 6 Science · Unit 2 of 10 · Elements and Chemical Changes · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** argue that mass is conserved in closed-system chemical changes at intro level.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout looking anything up, sketch what you think “physical change” means. Then star one part you are least sure about.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 2 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Conservation of Mass Intro** — sits inside **Elements and Chemical Changes**. Grade 6 science is not a pile of trivia; it is a set of models that help us explain patterns in matter, energy, Earth systems, and living things. A correct vocabulary word with no model or evidence is weaker than a clear diagram plus a checked explanation.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading weather maps, judging product claims, understanding local land and water, or talking about health and stewardship. At Prosper Prep we also connect careful science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show your model, cite evidence, and revise when new data arrives.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **physical change** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **nonmetal** — pair it with an example from a night sky watch from a dark parking lot.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “I observed… which supports…” over “because science says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. We want to understand something connected to “Conservation of Mass Intro.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Particle sketch, force diagram, Earth–Sun–Moon model, food web, data table, or systems map — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect observations to the definition of physical change. If numbers help (for example relative amounts 3 and -2), label units.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: treating density as the same as mass?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a night sky watch from a dark parking lot and labeled physical change and nonmetal. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (treating density as the same as mass…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Conservation of Mass Intro” but switch the context to a school recycling audit.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** treating density as the same as mass.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nPhysical science ideas about matter and change set up later force, energy, and Earth-system explanations.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a night sky watch from a dark parking lot, restate today’s goal (“Conservation of Mass Intro”) in your own words, then list two observations you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Argue that mass is conserved in closed-system chemical chang… Observations should be measurable or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **physical change** and **nonmetal** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a lab notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because of science” with no model. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear models + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Conservation of Mass Intro” to a short scenario about a family garden plot after rain. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 7, 4, 16.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Conservation of Mass Intro” to a short scenario about a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 9, 11, 37.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Conservation of Mass Intro” to a short scenario about a Prosper Prep science lab station. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 11, 10, 18.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Conservation of Mass Intro” to a short scenario about a kitchen density demo with oil and water. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 14, 11, 31.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Conservation of Mass Intro” to a short scenario about a USGS streamflow map for a Texas river. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 7, 10, 12.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Conservation of Mass Intro” to a short scenario about a creek-bank soil sample. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 9, 16, 33.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your model and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Argue that mass is conserved in closed-system chem…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Argue that mass is conserved in closed-system chem…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Argue that mass is conserved in closed-system chem…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Argue that mass is conserved in closed-system chem…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Argue that mass is conserved in closed-system chem…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Argue that mass is conserved in closed-system chem…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Conservation of Mass Intro” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: treating density as the same as…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Conservation of Mass Intro” using a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
