    content: "# Energy Unit Review\n\n*Grade 6 Science · Unit 4 of 10 · Energy · Lesson 8*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** mixed practice connecting KE, PE, transfers, and conservation language.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nTrue or false (guess OK): “saying seasons happen because Earth is closer to the Sun in summer” — write why you agree or disagree in one sentence.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 4 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Energy Unit Review** — sits inside **Energy**. Grade 6 science is not a pile of trivia; it is a set of models that help us explain patterns in matter, energy, Earth systems, and living things. A correct vocabulary word with no model or evidence is weaker than a clear diagram plus a checked explanation.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading weather maps, judging product claims, understanding local land and water, or talking about health and stewardship. At Prosper Prep we also connect careful science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show your model, cite evidence, and revise when new data arrives.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **potential energy** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **transfer** — pair it with an example from a night sky watch from a dark parking lot.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “I observed… which supports…” over “because science says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. We want to understand something connected to “Energy Unit Review.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Particle sketch, force diagram, Earth–Sun–Moon model, food web, data table, or systems map — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect observations to the definition of potential energy. If numbers help (for example relative amounts 4 and 0), label units.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: saying seasons happen because Earth is closer to the Sun in summer?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a night sky watch from a dark parking lot and labeled potential energy and transfer. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (saying seasons happen because Earth is closer to…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Energy Unit Review” but switch the context to a kitchen density demo with oil and water.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** saying seasons happen because Earth is closer to the Sun in summer.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nForces and energy explain *why* objects move and *how* changes happen without inventing energy from nowhere.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a night sky watch from a dark parking lot, restate today’s goal (“Energy Unit Review”) in your own words, then list two observations you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Mixed practice connecting KE, PE, transfers, and conservatio… Observations should be measurable or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **potential energy** and **transfer** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a lab notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because of science” with no model. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear models + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Energy Unit Review” to a short scenario about a USGS streamflow map for a Texas river. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 4, -2, 40.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Energy Unit Review” to a short scenario about a creek-bank soil sample. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 6, 5, 21.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Energy Unit Review” to a short scenario about a basketball on the gym floor. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 8, 4, 42.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Energy Unit Review” to a short scenario about a school recycling audit. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 11, 5, 15.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Energy Unit Review” to a short scenario about a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 13, 4, 36.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Energy Unit Review” to a short scenario about an East Texas piney woods trail near Tyler. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 15, 2, 17.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your model and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Mixed practice connecting KE, PE, transfers, and c…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Mixed practice connecting KE, PE, transfers, and c…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Mixed practice connecting KE, PE, transfers, and c…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Mixed practice connecting KE, PE, transfers, and c…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Mixed practice connecting KE, PE, transfers, and c…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Mixed practice connecting KE, PE, transfers, and c…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Energy Unit Review” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: saying seasons happen because Earth is…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Energy Unit Review” using a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
