    content: "# Tides Intro\n\n*Grade 6 Science · Unit 5 of 10 · Earth-Sun-Moon System · Lesson 6*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** connect tidal patterns to the Moon’s gravitational influence at an intro level.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at a family garden plot after rain. Write one question a scientist could ask that today’s idea (“Tides Intro”) might help answer.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 5 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Tides Intro** — sits inside **Earth-Sun-Moon System**. Grade 6 science is not a pile of trivia; it is a set of models that help us explain patterns in matter, energy, Earth systems, and living things. A correct vocabulary word with no model or evidence is weaker than a clear diagram plus a checked explanation.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading weather maps, judging product claims, understanding local land and water, or talking about health and stewardship. At Prosper Prep we also connect careful science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show your model, cite evidence, and revise when new data arrives.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **scale model** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **revolution** — pair it with an example from a family garden plot after rain.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “I observed… which supports…” over “because science says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a family garden plot after rain. We want to understand something connected to “Tides Intro.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Particle sketch, force diagram, Earth–Sun–Moon model, food web, data table, or systems map — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect observations to the definition of scale model. If numbers help (for example relative amounts 11 and 2), label units.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: claiming acquired skills (like riding a bike) are inherited genes?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a family garden plot after rain and labeled scale model and revolution. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (claiming acquired skills (like riding a bike) are…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Tides Intro” but switch the context to a kitchen density demo with oil and water.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** claiming acquired skills (like riding a bike) are inherited genes.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nEarth and space systems connect models of motion and matter to landscapes, resources, and stewardship.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a family garden plot after rain, restate today’s goal (“Tides Intro”) in your own words, then list two observations you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Connect tidal patterns to the Moon’s gravitational influence… Observations should be measurable or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **scale model** and **revolution** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a lab notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because of science” with no model. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear models + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Tides Intro” to a short scenario about a family garden plot after rain. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 5, -2, 28.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Tides Intro” to a short scenario about a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 7, 4, 49.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Tides Intro” to a short scenario about a Prosper Prep science lab station. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 9, 3, 30.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Tides Intro” to a short scenario about a creek-bank soil sample. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 11, 2, 11.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Tides Intro” to a short scenario about a basketball on the gym floor. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 13, 8, 32.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Tides Intro” to a short scenario about a school recycling audit. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 15, 7, 13.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your model and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Connect tidal patterns to the Moon’s gravitational…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Connect tidal patterns to the Moon’s gravitational…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Connect tidal patterns to the Moon’s gravitational…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Connect tidal patterns to the Moon’s gravitational…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Connect tidal patterns to the Moon’s gravitational…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Connect tidal patterns to the Moon’s gravitational…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Tides Intro” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: claiming acquired skills (like riding a…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Tides Intro” using a family garden plot after rain. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
