    content: "# Water Cycle as a System\n\n*Grade 6 Science · Unit 6 of 10 · Earth’s Systems and Structure · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** trace water through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout looking anything up, sketch what you think “geosphere” means. Then star one part you are least sure about.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 6 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Water Cycle as a System** — sits inside **Earth’s Systems and Structure**. Grade 6 science is not a pile of trivia; it is a set of models that help us explain patterns in matter, energy, Earth systems, and living things. A correct vocabulary word with no model or evidence is weaker than a clear diagram plus a checked explanation.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading weather maps, judging product claims, understanding local land and water, or talking about health and stewardship. At Prosper Prep we also connect careful science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show your model, cite evidence, and revise when new data arrives.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **geosphere** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **plate** — pair it with an example from a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “I observed… which supports…” over “because science says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout. We want to understand something connected to “Water Cycle as a System.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Particle sketch, force diagram, Earth–Sun–Moon model, food web, data table, or systems map — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect observations to the definition of geosphere. If numbers help (for example relative amounts 3 and -3), label units.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: calling every bubbling change a chemical change without other evidence?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout and labeled geosphere and plate. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (calling every bubbling change a chemical change without…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Water Cycle as a System” but switch the context to a family garden plot after rain.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** calling every bubbling change a chemical change without other evidence.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nEarth and space systems connect models of motion and matter to landscapes, resources, and stewardship.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout, restate today’s goal (“Water Cycle as a System”) in your own words, then list two observations you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Trace water through evaporation, condensation, precipitation… Observations should be measurable or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **geosphere** and **plate** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a lab notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because of science” with no model. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear models + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Water Cycle as a System” to a short scenario about a kitchen density demo with oil and water. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 5, 9, 41.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Water Cycle as a System” to a short scenario about a Prosper Prep science lab station. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 5, 5, 20.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Water Cycle as a System” to a short scenario about a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 5, 8, 39.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Water Cycle as a System” to a short scenario about a family garden plot after rain. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 13, 9, 26.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Water Cycle as a System” to a short scenario about an East Texas piney woods trail near Tyler. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 13, 12, 45.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Water Cycle as a System” to a short scenario about a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 13, 15, 24.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your model and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Trace water through evaporation, condensation, pre…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Trace water through evaporation, condensation, pre…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Trace water through evaporation, condensation, pre…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Trace water through evaporation, condensation, pre…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Trace water through evaporation, condensation, pre…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Trace water through evaporation, condensation, pre…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Water Cycle as a System” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: calling every bubbling change a chemical…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Water Cycle as a System” using a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
