    content: "# Ecosystem Changes and Stability\n\n*Grade 6 Science · Unit 8 of 10 · Interactions in Ecosystems · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** predict how removing a key species or resource can ripple through a web.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at an East Texas piney woods trail near Tyler. Write one question a scientist could ask that today’s idea (“Ecosystem Changes and Stability”) might help answer.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 8 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Ecosystem Changes and Stability** — sits inside **Interactions in Ecosystems**. Grade 6 science is not a pile of trivia; it is a set of models that help us explain patterns in matter, energy, Earth systems, and living things. A correct vocabulary word with no model or evidence is weaker than a clear diagram plus a checked explanation.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading weather maps, judging product claims, understanding local land and water, or talking about health and stewardship. At Prosper Prep we also connect careful science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show your model, cite evidence, and revise when new data arrives.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **decomposer** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **food web** — pair it with an example from an East Texas piney woods trail near Tyler.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “I observed… which supports…” over “because science says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine an East Texas piney woods trail near Tyler. We want to understand something connected to “Ecosystem Changes and Stability.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Particle sketch, force diagram, Earth–Sun–Moon model, food web, data table, or systems map — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect observations to the definition of decomposer. If numbers help (for example relative amounts 9 and 8), label units.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: treating models as exact miniature Earths with perfect distances?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of an East Texas piney woods trail near Tyler and labeled decomposer and food web. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (treating models as exact miniature Earths with perfect…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Ecosystem Changes and Stability” but switch the context to a USGS streamflow map for a Texas river.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** treating models as exact miniature Earths with perfect distances.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nLife science builds on matter/energy: organisms are systems that transform energy and pass on traits in environments.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of an East Texas piney woods trail near Tyler, restate today’s goal (“Ecosystem Changes and Stability”) in your own words, then list two observations you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Predict how removing a key species or resource can ripple th… Observations should be measurable or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **decomposer** and **food web** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a lab notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because of science” with no model. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear models + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Ecosystem Changes and Stability” to a short scenario about a school recycling audit. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 7, 0, 49.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Ecosystem Changes and Stability” to a short scenario about a basketball on the gym floor. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 7, 4, 28.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Ecosystem Changes and Stability” to a short scenario about a creek-bank soil sample. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 7, -1, 47.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Ecosystem Changes and Stability” to a short scenario about a USGS streamflow map for a Texas river. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 6, 0, 34.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Ecosystem Changes and Stability” to a short scenario about a kitchen density demo with oil and water. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 15, 3, 13.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Ecosystem Changes and Stability” to a short scenario about a Prosper Prep science lab station. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 15, 6, 32.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your model and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Predict how removing a key species or resource can…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Predict how removing a key species or resource can…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Predict how removing a key species or resource can…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Predict how removing a key species or resource can…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Predict how removing a key species or resource can…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Predict how removing a key species or resource can…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Ecosystem Changes and Stability” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: treating models as exact miniature Earths…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Ecosystem Changes and Stability” using an East Texas piney woods trail near Tyler. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
