    content: "# Organization: Cells to Systems\n\n*Grade 6 Science · Unit 9 of 10 · Cells and Organisms · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** sequence cells → tissues → organs → systems with a human or plant example.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students use models, vocabulary, and evidence. Prefer labeled diagrams and CER sentences over one-word answers.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nTrue or false (guess OK): “saying energy is “used up” instead of transferred or transformed” — write why you agree or disagree in one sentence.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior idea from earlier in Unit 9 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday’s skill — **Organization: Cells to Systems** — sits inside **Cells and Organisms**. Grade 6 science is not a pile of trivia; it is a set of models that help us explain patterns in matter, energy, Earth systems, and living things. A correct vocabulary word with no model or evidence is weaker than a clear diagram plus a checked explanation.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when reading weather maps, judging product claims, understanding local land and water, or talking about health and stewardship. At Prosper Prep we also connect careful science habits to scholarship habits: define terms, show your model, cite evidence, and revise when new data arrives.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **system** — use this word with a definition in your own sentence, not as decoration.\n- **photosynthesis** — pair it with an example from a night sky watch from a dark parking lot.\n- **Evidence** beats slogans. Prefer “I observed… which supports…” over “because science says so.”\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. We want to understand something connected to “Organization: Cells to Systems.”\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we trying to explain or decide?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** Particle sketch, force diagram, Earth–Sun–Moon model, food web, data table, or systems map — pick what matches the question.\n**Step 3 — Apply the science idea.** Connect observations to the definition of system. If numbers help (for example relative amounts 7 and -2), label units.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Ask: Does this contradict a known pattern? Did we confuse a common mistake like: saying energy is “used up” instead of transferred or transformed?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I defined the key terms first. I drew a quick model of a night sky watch from a dark parking lot and labeled system and photosynthesis. My evidence was that when conditions changed, the pattern matched the lesson prediction. I checked for the common mix-up (saying energy is “used up” instead of transferred…) and revised my labels so a classmate could follow the reasoning.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nKeep the mathematical/scientific structure of “Organization: Cells to Systems” but switch the context to a creek-bank soil sample.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Write two evidence sentences.\n4. Write a concluding claim that a skeptic could test.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature vocabulary.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** saying energy is “used up” instead of transferred or transformed.\n- **Also watch for:** copying a diagram without labels; treating a model as reality; confusing correlation with a full causal explanation.\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire explanation blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nLife science builds on matter/energy: organisms are systems that transform energy and pass on traits in environments.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — In the context of a night sky watch from a dark parking lot, restate today’s goal (“Organization: Cells to Systems”) in your own words, then list two observations you would collect.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Goal restates Sequence cells → tissues → organs → systems with a human or … Observations should be measurable or clearly describable with units/labels.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Use the terms **system** and **photosynthesis** correctly in two sentences that could appear in a lab notebook.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Sentences show accurate definitions and connect to the lesson phenomenon; no circular “it is what it is.”\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student wrote only “because of science” with no model. What two follow-up questions should you ask before accepting the answer?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear models + evidence sentences.\n\n1. Problem 1: Apply “Organization: Cells to Systems” to a short scenario about a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 6, 10, 26.)\n2. Problem 2: Apply “Organization: Cells to Systems” to a short scenario about a creek-bank soil sample. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 4, 10, 43.)\n3. Problem 3: Apply “Organization: Cells to Systems” to a short scenario about a basketball on the gym floor. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 6, 8, 24.)\n4. Problem 4: Apply “Organization: Cells to Systems” to a short scenario about a NASA Moon-phase calendar printout. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 13, 8, 41.)\n5. Problem 5: Apply “Organization: Cells to Systems” to a short scenario about a Prosper Prep science lab station. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 15, 15, 22.)\n6. Problem 6: Apply “Organization: Cells to Systems” to a short scenario about an East Texas piney woods trail near Tyler. Include a labeled sketch or table and one evidence sentence. (Optional numbers to use if helpful: 13, 15, 39.)\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your model and evidence, not only a final word.*\n\n1. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Sequence cells → tissues → organs → systems with a…\n2. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Sequence cells → tissues → organs → systems with a…\n3. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Sequence cells → tissues → organs → systems with a…\n4. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Sequence cells → tissues → organs → systems with a…\n5. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Sequence cells → tissues → organs → systems with a…\n6. Strong responses define key terms, show a representation, and cite evidence tied to Sequence cells → tissues → organs → systems with a…\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Organization: Cells to Systems” let you explain that you could not explain as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint related to: saying energy is “used up” instead…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) about “Organization: Cells to Systems” using a night sky watch from a dark parking lot. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
